  ],
  "slug": "confidence-ordering-contextual-68b5",
  "summary": "Ab initio, network-entropy and coarse-grained simulation methods now predict functional RNA and protein behavior at genome and residue scale.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is computational prediction of molecular function, with explicit validation in some cases. AbRiDGed, an ab initio method, predicts millions of self-cleaving ribozymes across species and identifies roughly 150 novel classes. A discrete-time quantum walk approach with Shannon entropy on protein contact networks localizes residues involved in long-range interactions and is validated on AsLOV2. The DDX3X study combines coarse-grained simulations with deep learning to describe ATP-independent, entropy-driven dsRNA unwinding through rare strand-displacing intermediates. The validation orientation is dominant rather than universal, since the DDX3X work is primarily mechanistic. What is new is the use of network-entropy walks to localize communication residues and genome-scale ab initio discovery of catalytic RNAs.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-03"
}
